WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.280
I want you to imagine, just for a second, um, trying to reconstruct your entire music library

00:05.280 --> 00:10.460
using only the physical objects that will be left in your house a hundred thousand years from now.

00:10.940 --> 00:14.300
Oh man, that is, uh, that is quite the timeline.

00:14.520 --> 00:16.380
Right, think about that, a hundred millennia.

00:17.040 --> 00:22.220
No digital files, your mp3s, your streaming playlists, your hard drives, those are all just gone in a blink.

00:22.740 --> 00:26.280
The servers rust away completely, the data just degrades.

00:26.520 --> 00:29.340
Exactly. The magnetic tape turns to dust.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.300
And no wood from your acoustic guitar either, no plastic from your noise-canceling headphones,

00:35.300 --> 00:38.280
no paper sheet music, and definitely no vinyl records.

00:38.940 --> 00:41.160
The organic world reclaims literally all of it.

00:41.320 --> 00:47.160
Yes. So after a hundred thousand years, essentially the only things left of your entire musical existence

00:47.160 --> 00:49.260
will be incredibly durable materials.

00:49.640 --> 00:55.200
Stuff like stone, or maybe some really thick ceramics or hyper-dense glass.

00:56.140 --> 00:58.800
It is a sobering thought experiment.

00:58.800 --> 01:04.460
You are asking future archaeologists to basically look at a pile of rocks and infer a symphony.

01:05.100 --> 01:08.840
Exactly. How accurately could those future scientists recreate your favorite songs?

01:09.640 --> 01:14.640
Or, I mean, taking it a step further, how could they even prove that you liked music at all,

01:14.780 --> 01:19.860
based purely on a few weirdly shaped chunks of masonry sitting where your living room used to be?

01:19.860 --> 01:21.520
They probably couldn't.

01:21.520 --> 01:27.120
I mean, they might look at a dense ceramic mug and say, uh, look, a percussion instrument.

01:27.600 --> 01:32.080
Right. Or they might look at a heavy glass paperweight and just assume it was used for grinding grain.

01:32.420 --> 01:40.600
It is a virtually impossible task, but it perfectly illustrates the central, frankly, agonizing problem of prehistoric music.

01:40.600 --> 01:43.320
Sound just doesn't fossilize.

01:44.020 --> 01:44.900
It really doesn't.

01:45.720 --> 01:50.980
The physical vibration of air is like the most transient human experience imaginable.

01:51.520 --> 01:53.000
It doesn't fossilize.

01:53.360 --> 01:59.220
And the behavioral practices surrounding it are equally invisible to the archaeological record.

01:59.740 --> 02:02.920
The singing, the clapping, the rhythmic stomping on dirt.

02:02.920 --> 02:05.320
The drumming, unstretched animal hides.

02:05.740 --> 02:18.980
Yeah, exactly. All of those actions leave absolutely zero durable trace, unless they happen to involve a very specific, very tough object that miraculously survives the crushing weight of geological time.

02:19.220 --> 02:24.280
We are essentially trying to reconstruct a ghost from the shadows it's supposedly cast.

02:24.640 --> 02:26.040
That is a great way to put it.

02:26.300 --> 02:29.300
Which brings us to the grounding material for today's deep dive.

02:29.300 --> 02:34.220
We are looking at a brilliantly provocative paper from October 2026.

02:34.500 --> 02:35.280
It's a fascinating read.

02:35.360 --> 02:35.960
It really is.

02:36.020 --> 02:41.280
It's by an independent researcher named Flyxion, titled StonePianoHistory.pdf.

02:41.480 --> 02:45.840
And our mission today is to explore something called the Stone Piano Hypothesis.

02:45.920 --> 02:48.240
Which sounds completely wild when you first hear it.

02:48.400 --> 02:49.900
It sounds like science fiction.

02:50.420 --> 02:57.720
It asks if early Neanderthals built acoustic instruments out of cave stalagmites over 170,000 years ago.

02:57.720 --> 02:58.840
A massive claim.

02:59.300 --> 03:06.360
Right. And more importantly, because anyone can just throw out a wild theory, how in the world could we possibly prove it using modern science?

03:06.760 --> 03:07.940
That is the real trick.

03:08.420 --> 03:09.480
Okay, let's unpack this.

03:09.660 --> 03:17.620
Because trying to read the archaeological record of human music feels like trying to read a thousand-page novel where 99% of the pages have been burned.

03:18.100 --> 03:21.120
And the surviving pages are written in a language we barely understand.

03:21.120 --> 03:23.300
The inference gap is just massive.

03:23.920 --> 03:30.860
Every single claim we make about ancient music is, at its core, a leap of faith grounded in whatever sparse data we can scrape together.

03:31.120 --> 03:36.560
We are drawing sweeping cultural conclusions from a tiny set of surviving objects.

03:36.780 --> 03:39.140
Vastly unrepresentative objects, yes.

03:39.140 --> 03:47.660
And what Flyxion emphasizes right away in this text is that the exact same object, for instance, a ring of broken stones found deep inside a cave,

03:48.160 --> 03:54.380
well, it can support completely different claims depending entirely on the assumptions the researcher brings to the table.

03:54.380 --> 03:57.800
So one person sees one thing, another sees something else.

03:57.960 --> 04:03.440
Exactly. One researcher looks at a ring of stones and sees a hearth enclosure, you know, just to keep a fire contained.

04:03.620 --> 04:04.060
Makes sense.

04:04.400 --> 04:07.060
Another sees a territorial boundary or a windbreak.

04:07.640 --> 04:09.500
Another sees a ritualistic altar.

04:10.280 --> 04:15.400
And someone else looks at those exact same stones, measures them, and sees a giant shattered xylophone.

04:16.560 --> 04:17.760
A stone piano.

04:18.020 --> 04:18.240
Right.

04:18.660 --> 04:20.280
The rocks themselves are silent.

04:20.800 --> 04:23.580
The human interpretation is what actually gives them a voice.

04:23.580 --> 04:28.720
And that's exactly why this isn't just going to be a dry archaeological report for you as you listen today.

04:28.820 --> 04:30.700
We are going to dig into the mud here.

04:31.120 --> 04:31.980
We really have to.

04:32.720 --> 04:38.900
This deep dive is going to be a masterclass in critical thinking, in the literal physics of how sound moves through solid matter,

04:39.360 --> 04:45.380
and in giving you those ultimate aha moments about how modern science manages to test the seemingly untestable.

04:45.840 --> 04:50.540
Because before we can even talk about Neanderthals playing stone pianos in the dark,

04:50.540 --> 04:53.580
we really have to understand the ground rules of the discipline.

04:54.320 --> 04:57.840
We have to understand how scientists actually grade the evidence of ancient music.

04:58.160 --> 04:58.420
Right.

04:58.940 --> 05:03.180
Why are some objects instantly celebrated as ancient instruments on the cover of magazines,

05:03.400 --> 05:08.480
while others spark decades of bitter, almost hostile academic debate?

05:08.480 --> 05:12.020
It comes down to establishing a hierarchy of certainty, doesn't it?

05:12.340 --> 05:12.660
It does.

05:13.040 --> 05:14.660
Not all evidence is created equal.

05:15.160 --> 05:21.200
And treating a faint possibility as a proven fact is the fastest way to derail archaeological science.

05:21.460 --> 05:22.820
You just lose all credibility.

05:23.200 --> 05:23.560
Exactly.

05:24.380 --> 05:29.580
Flyxion structures this perfectly by dividing the existing evidence into four distinct grades.

05:30.340 --> 05:35.380
You absolutely have to know what grade of evidence you are dealing with before you can evaluate a historical claim.

05:35.380 --> 05:39.520
Because if you treat grade four evidence like grade one evidence...

05:39.520 --> 05:40.960
Your entire argument just collapses.

05:41.260 --> 05:41.480
Right.

05:42.020 --> 05:43.640
So let's start at the top of the mountain.

05:43.880 --> 05:44.440
Grade one.

05:44.600 --> 05:45.720
The undisputed champions.

05:46.100 --> 05:48.380
The holy grail of acoustic archaeology.

05:48.740 --> 05:53.040
These are the objects where practically everyone in the global scientific community looks at them,

05:53.280 --> 05:56.980
runs the test, and says, yep, without a shadow of a doubt, that makes me sick.

05:57.120 --> 05:59.320
And the details of these objects are just incredible.

05:59.880 --> 06:00.540
They really are.

06:00.540 --> 06:05.600
We are talking primarily about the Swabian alb flutes from southwestern Germany.

06:06.240 --> 06:13.940
At a site called Geisen Klosterly, archaeologists found flutes made from mute swan bone and from mammoth ivory.

06:14.240 --> 06:15.360
Mammoth ivory.

06:16.020 --> 06:17.560
Just think about the effort that takes.

06:18.680 --> 06:26.620
And using high-resolution radiocarbon dating, they placed these artifacts at roughly 42,000 to 43,000 years old.

06:26.820 --> 06:27.360
Incredible.

06:27.360 --> 06:34.420
Then, at another nearby site called Holafels, they found a flute made from the radius bone of a griffin vulture.

06:35.020 --> 06:41.740
It has five distinct carefully carved finger holes, and that one is dated to somewhere between 35,000 and 40,000 years ago.

06:42.000 --> 06:44.840
Those are spectacular paradigm-shifting finds.

06:45.260 --> 06:50.100
But what is crucial for our listener to understand is why they occupy that grade one status.

06:50.360 --> 06:53.660
It's not just because they are old bones with holes in them, right?

06:53.660 --> 06:58.120
Exactly. It is the undeniable intentionality of their creation.

06:59.020 --> 07:01.940
They are deliberately and painstakingly worked.

07:02.580 --> 07:12.740
The mammoth ivory flute, for example, it had to be split in half, completely hollowed out, carved with finger holes, and then bound back together perfectly to create an airtight seal.

07:13.000 --> 07:14.120
You don't do that by accident.

07:14.320 --> 07:14.960
You really don't.

07:14.960 --> 07:21.620
And the finger holes on the vulture bone are regularly spaced in a way that is mathematically optimal for sound production.

07:22.000 --> 07:22.180
Wow.

07:23.060 --> 07:30.800
Furthermore, they are found in archaeological contexts that also contain early figurative art, like the famous Venus of Holtfelts.

07:30.800 --> 07:33.460
So it's not just an isolated, weird bone.

07:33.880 --> 07:35.660
It's part of a cultural explosion.

07:35.960 --> 07:36.060
Right.

07:36.220 --> 07:36.980
You have sculpture.

07:37.240 --> 07:37.840
You have music.

07:38.060 --> 07:45.760
You have a society that is investing immense time and caloric energy into things that do not directly contribute to hunting or gathering.

07:46.100 --> 07:46.580
Exactly.

07:47.000 --> 07:49.560
There is a whole cultural package there.

07:49.560 --> 08:00.840
But, and this is a massive limitation that the text insists we remember, this only proves that deliberate music existed in this specific region and at this specific time.

08:01.560 --> 08:04.740
It does not mean music originated in southwestern Germany.

08:04.980 --> 08:05.240
Right.

08:05.380 --> 08:08.740
And it certainly doesn't prove it existed everywhere else on the globe at that time.

08:08.740 --> 08:19.660
It just proves with absolute certainty that 40,000 years ago, anatomically modern humans in the Swabian Alp were carving intricate wind instruments.

08:20.120 --> 08:25.120
Which brings us to the much murkier waters of grade 2, the disputed objects.

08:25.440 --> 08:26.640
This is where the fighting happens.

08:27.520 --> 08:31.900
Let me introduce you to the Divye-Babe cave bear femur from Slovenia.

08:32.580 --> 08:37.580
Now, initially, researchers radiocarbon dated this bone to about 43,000 years ago.

08:37.580 --> 08:39.400
But then they retested it.

08:39.640 --> 08:39.800
Right.

08:40.360 --> 08:44.280
Later, they used a different method called electron spin resonance.

08:44.800 --> 08:49.800
And that pushed the date way back to 50,000, maybe even 60,000 years ago.

08:49.900 --> 08:51.300
Which is incredibly old.

08:51.800 --> 08:52.640
It's a bone.

08:53.240 --> 08:53.780
It's hollow.

08:54.200 --> 08:55.140
It has holes in it.

08:55.340 --> 09:00.160
So I'm going to play devil's advocate here because if I'm looking at a photo of this thing, it looks like a flute.

09:00.580 --> 09:02.980
It does look very convincing to the naked eye.

09:02.980 --> 09:09.280
If it's a hollow bone and it has multiple spaced holes in it, why is there a debate?

09:09.440 --> 09:12.940
Isn't a bone with holes obviously a flute?

09:13.840 --> 09:16.100
What's fascinating here is the alternate hypothesis.

09:16.800 --> 09:19.520
And it's an incredibly compelling, heavily researched one.

09:20.160 --> 09:21.020
Carnivore chewing.

09:21.580 --> 09:21.860
Wait.

09:22.300 --> 09:23.940
Like animals biting it?

09:24.220 --> 09:24.680
Exactly.

09:25.140 --> 09:28.540
Think about the ecosystem of a Slovenian cave 50,000 years ago.

09:28.540 --> 09:31.140
Cave bears lived and died in these spaces.

09:31.780 --> 09:36.540
And their bones were scavenged by apex predators, specifically wolves, cave lions, and hyenas.

09:37.280 --> 09:37.400
Okay.

09:37.500 --> 09:39.060
Hyenas have crazy bite force.

09:39.520 --> 09:40.480
Massive bite force.

09:41.040 --> 09:48.140
If you look at the jaw mechanics of a hyena, their canine teeth apply focused crushing pressure to crack bones for marrow.

09:48.740 --> 09:50.920
So a tooth punches through the bone.

09:51.360 --> 09:51.640
Right.

09:52.020 --> 09:57.560
Puncture marks from a large carnivore's canine teeth can look remarkably similar to a deliberately drilled finger hole.

09:57.560 --> 09:58.240
Oh, wow.

09:58.320 --> 09:58.500
Really?

09:58.880 --> 10:04.320
Especially after 50,000 years of soil degradation, water dripping, and mineral incrustation.

10:05.080 --> 10:11.260
If a human uses a stone tool to bore a hole, they leave microscopic circular striations on the rim of the hole.

10:11.540 --> 10:12.700
Like drill marks.

10:13.000 --> 10:13.260
Yes.

10:13.840 --> 10:19.000
But if a hyena bites through a bone, the bone splinters inward, creating microfractures.

10:19.000 --> 10:27.360
The problem with the Divya Babi femur is that the edges are so degraded that definitive microscopic proof of human tooling is hotly contested.

10:27.680 --> 10:30.740
So people are just arguing over whether it's a drill mark or a bite mark.

10:31.100 --> 10:31.840
Basically, yes.

10:32.260 --> 10:37.700
So the Divya Babi bone cannot support a definitive grade one claim that it is a flute.

10:38.140 --> 10:41.960
What it supports, according to the text, is a conditional claim.

10:41.960 --> 10:45.980
A conditional claim, meaning an if-then statement.

10:46.280 --> 10:46.380
Right.

10:46.380 --> 10:48.000
We can't say it is a flute.

10:48.100 --> 10:48.980
We have to say if.

10:49.500 --> 10:49.860
Exactly.

10:50.520 --> 11:01.080
If it is an instrument, then it is a Neanderthal instrument because of that 50,000 to 60,000 year timeline, which predates the widespread arrival of Homo sapiens in that specific area.

11:01.460 --> 11:03.460
It would be a monumental discovery.

11:03.460 --> 11:06.640
It would prove Neanderthals had advanced musical culture.

11:07.640 --> 11:15.260
But until someone can conclusively, undeniably prove those holes were made by sharp stone tools and not by the crushing bite of a starving hyena.

11:15.620 --> 11:17.480
It stays firmly in grade two?

11:17.840 --> 11:18.160
Yes.

11:18.700 --> 11:22.580
You have to carry that massive if into any argument you make about it.

11:23.160 --> 11:28.860
You can't base your entire theory of Neanderthal cognition on a bone that might just be a hyena's leftover lunch.

11:29.460 --> 11:31.160
Okay, so that establishes the top tier.

11:31.160 --> 11:33.180
Grade one is undisputed proof.

11:33.740 --> 11:38.420
Grade two is a disputed physical object where the origin of the modification is debated.

11:39.120 --> 11:41.300
Now we get to grade three and grade four.

11:41.640 --> 11:44.520
Which is where the archaeology of sound gets truly abstract.

11:44.920 --> 11:46.940
And where our stone piano really lives.

11:47.280 --> 11:48.200
Let's start with grade three.

11:48.860 --> 11:57.500
This is defined as structures, assemblages, or spatial arrangements, where an acoustic purpose is just one of several completely valid hypotheses.

11:58.160 --> 12:05.340
We'll dive deep into Brunickel Cave in a second, which is the ultimate grade three example, a physical structure that might be an instrument or might be a wall.

12:05.840 --> 12:08.640
But let's round out the framework with grade four first.

12:09.460 --> 12:10.760
Grade four is the most abstract.

12:10.760 --> 12:14.680
It consists entirely of indirect inferences and correlations.

12:15.160 --> 12:15.600
Like what?

12:15.600 --> 12:23.820
Well, this involves things like the acoustic studies conducted by researchers like Reznikov and Dovois, or later by Fazenda and his colleagues.

12:23.820 --> 12:30.300
They went into upper Paleolithic caves in France and Spain, and they mapped out the acoustics of the cave systems.

12:31.040 --> 12:32.280
Just testing the echoes and stuff?

12:32.440 --> 12:32.580
Right.

12:32.580 --> 12:42.800
And they found that the locations of ancient cave paintings, the densest clusters of art, often correlate with the places in the cave that have the absolute highest acoustic resonance.

12:43.660 --> 12:44.400
Oh, that's wild.

12:45.060 --> 12:50.120
The places with the best echoes, the longest reverberations, are the places with the most paintings.

12:50.960 --> 12:53.240
Grade four also includes ethnographic analogies.

12:53.720 --> 12:55.880
Which means looking at modern people.

12:55.880 --> 13:02.900
Yes. Observing modern human cultures, say in parts of Africa or Asia, that actively use rock gongs.

13:03.420 --> 13:04.320
Rock gongs.

13:05.020 --> 13:08.900
Naturally resonant boulders that communities strike for communication or ritual.

13:09.480 --> 13:09.780
I see.

13:10.080 --> 13:14.980
So if I find a modern culture using a rock gong, I can say, look, humans do this.

13:15.080 --> 13:15.200
Right.

13:15.340 --> 13:20.300
And if I find a painting in an echoey cave, I can say, look, they cared about sound.

13:20.440 --> 13:23.540
But I don't have the actual instrument in either case.

13:23.640 --> 13:24.040
Precisely.

13:24.040 --> 13:25.780
It tells you what to look for.

13:26.260 --> 13:31.480
And it establishes that hitting rocks to make sound is a recurrent, physically viable human practice.

13:32.240 --> 13:38.320
But finding a cave painting in an echo chamber doesn't prove a specific person stood there and sang a specific song.

13:38.780 --> 13:40.420
It just sets up a plausibility.

13:41.160 --> 13:46.380
And this is such a vital takeaway for you as a listener, really for navigating modern media.

13:47.200 --> 13:53.040
When you are reading the news and you see a massive clickbait headline about some ancient discovery.

13:53.040 --> 13:55.920
Scientists prove Neanderthals sang to cave paintings.

13:56.000 --> 13:56.460
Exactly.

13:57.380 --> 14:01.520
Pay close attention to the grade of the evidence in the actual article.

14:02.360 --> 14:05.140
Are they talking about a grade one flute with finger holes?

14:05.620 --> 14:11.420
Or are they talking about a grade four correlation between a drawing of a bison and a nice echo?

14:12.040 --> 14:14.700
An analogy or a correlation shows what is possible.

14:15.100 --> 14:16.880
It does not prove what actually happened.

14:16.880 --> 14:22.300
It is the difference between proving a suspect had a motive and proving they actually committed the crime.

14:22.660 --> 14:24.740
Grade four gives you the motive and the opportunity.

14:25.460 --> 14:27.040
It doesn't give you the smoking gun.

14:27.580 --> 14:27.740
All right.

14:27.780 --> 14:32.340
With our strict grades of evidence firmly established, let's descend into the dark.

14:32.620 --> 14:37.400
We are moving from these general categories directly into the ultimate grade three test case.

14:37.860 --> 14:38.820
The Bruniqual Cave.

14:38.820 --> 14:41.260
Yes, in Occitania in southwestern France.

14:41.920 --> 14:49.360
I want to set the physical scene for you here because the facts of this site, just the raw, undeniable measurements, are absolutely staggering.

14:49.940 --> 14:50.480
They really are.

14:50.760 --> 15:01.460
Even without the acoustic hypothesis, Bruniqual challenges almost everything we previously assumed about the engineering capabilities and social organization of early Neanderthals.

15:01.460 --> 15:05.020
So this site was discovered relatively recently, right, in 1990?

15:05.460 --> 15:05.720
Yes.

15:06.160 --> 15:13.020
A team of speleologists dug through a blocked entrance and found themselves in a massive, untouched underground network.

15:13.320 --> 15:18.660
The structures we are talking about are located 336 meters deep into the cave.

15:18.740 --> 15:20.240
I want you to really visualize that.

15:21.660 --> 15:22.580
336 meters.

15:23.060 --> 15:29.440
That is over three football fields long, winding through treacherous, uneven, damp limestone tunnels.

15:29.440 --> 15:32.660
It is completely removed from any natural light.

15:32.780 --> 15:37.060
If your torch goes out, you are in absolute sensory deprivation darkness.

15:37.580 --> 15:43.500
And the structures built inside this deep chamber were dated using a highly reliable method called uranium series dating.

15:43.720 --> 15:47.060
They tested the stalagmite regrowth over the structures, right?

15:47.240 --> 15:47.600
Exactly.

15:47.980 --> 15:54.720
And they pinpointed the construction to roughly 176,500 years ago, plus or minus a couple of thousand years.

15:54.720 --> 16:00.220
Which places it squarely, undeniably, in the era of early Neanderthals.

16:00.580 --> 16:06.380
This is tens of thousands of years before anatomically modern Homo sapiens even set foot in Europe.

16:06.660 --> 16:08.360
It completely shatters the timeline.

16:08.840 --> 16:12.220
And we have to look at what they actually built down there in the pitch black.

16:12.340 --> 16:14.100
They didn't just pile up some loose gravel.

16:14.460 --> 16:18.480
They deliberately broke off roughly 400 stalagmite pieces.

16:18.880 --> 16:20.260
Yeah, that part is mind-blowing.

16:20.260 --> 16:24.560
I have to stop you there, actually, because when I read that, it broke my brain.

16:25.100 --> 16:26.700
400 stalagmites.

16:27.220 --> 16:29.200
Have you ever tried to break a stalagmite?

16:29.820 --> 16:32.480
It requires immense kinetic force.

16:32.860 --> 16:34.400
Calcite is incredibly dense.

16:35.220 --> 16:37.120
And they didn't just randomly smash them.

16:37.560 --> 16:41.720
The combined length of these 400 fragments is about 112 meters.

16:42.420 --> 16:46.220
And the estimated total weight of the stone moved is 2.2 tons.

16:46.400 --> 16:47.320
2.2 tons.

16:47.320 --> 16:49.840
This wasn't a casual afternoon project.

16:50.400 --> 16:52.880
This wasn't one guy wandering in to get out of the rain.

16:53.640 --> 16:58.820
The Neanderthals arranged these stalagmite pieces into distinct, carefully constructed rings.

16:59.000 --> 17:00.900
Like actual geometric shapes.

17:01.280 --> 17:02.040
They're T-main rings.

17:02.580 --> 17:07.440
A large one, about 6.7 by 4.5 meters, built with multiple layers of stone.

17:07.820 --> 17:10.500
And a smaller one, about 2.2 by 2.1 meters.

17:10.920 --> 17:11.140
Okay.

17:11.600 --> 17:14.940
There are also smaller accumulations of stone inside and outside the rings.

17:14.940 --> 17:20.520
And across all six of these structural groupings, there are undeniable signs of fire.

17:20.660 --> 17:23.580
We see reddened and blackened pieces of stone.

17:23.780 --> 17:24.520
We see soot.

17:24.920 --> 17:27.280
And we see fractured stone caused by intense heat.

17:27.620 --> 17:30.980
They were deliberately building, maintaining, and controlling fires down there.

17:30.980 --> 17:33.100
So let's review the facts for a second.

17:34.440 --> 17:35.780
336 meters underground.

17:36.440 --> 17:40.320
2.2 tons of rock deliberately broken and transported.

17:40.980 --> 17:43.000
Complex, geometric rings.

17:45.040 --> 17:46.820
176,000 years ago.

17:47.060 --> 17:47.920
It's a lot to take in.

17:48.160 --> 17:48.580
It is.

17:48.580 --> 17:51.780
So, the raw facts prove deliberate construction.

17:52.460 --> 17:55.100
They prove the sustained use of fire deep underground.

17:55.100 --> 18:00.420
And they prove incredible spatial planning and group organization by early Neanderthals.

18:00.920 --> 18:05.600
There is no natural process that creates interlocking rings of burnt stalagmites.

18:05.880 --> 18:06.000
None.

18:06.560 --> 18:07.700
But why they did it?

18:08.120 --> 18:10.020
The interpretations are all over the map.

18:10.020 --> 18:12.880
Which is exactly why this is a grade 3 mystery.

18:13.180 --> 18:15.920
The object is undeniably real and anthropogenic.

18:16.380 --> 18:18.820
But the purpose is entirely up for debate.

18:19.620 --> 18:22.380
If you look at the standard hypotheses in the archaeological literature,

18:22.900 --> 18:25.380
some suggest maybe it was a temporary refuge.

18:26.060 --> 18:26.440
But wait.

18:26.520 --> 18:28.520
If it's a refuge, where's the trash?

18:28.680 --> 18:30.460
Where are the animal bones from their meals?

18:30.520 --> 18:30.960
Exactly.

18:31.140 --> 18:33.760
Where are the discarded stone tools they use to scrape hides?

18:34.080 --> 18:36.180
Usually a dwelling is full of domestic debris.

18:36.800 --> 18:38.140
Precisely the counter-argument.

18:38.140 --> 18:42.280
The floor of Brinicwell is remarkably clean of domestic refuse.

18:42.920 --> 18:43.520
Okay.

18:43.640 --> 18:44.700
So, probably not a house.

18:45.500 --> 18:49.300
Others suggest the rings were a social or symbolic boundary.

18:49.680 --> 18:52.820
A way of demarcating sacred space from profane space.

18:53.680 --> 18:55.680
Or maybe it was a ritual setting.

18:56.280 --> 18:59.280
Specifically chosen because the sensory deprivation of the dark,

18:59.760 --> 19:01.500
combined with the flickering firelight,

19:01.620 --> 19:03.240
created an altered state of consciousness.

19:03.920 --> 19:06.440
But the paper we are looking at by Flyxion

19:06.440 --> 19:10.120
elevates a totally different, vastly more complex interpretation.

19:10.820 --> 19:13.260
The stone piano hypothesis.

19:13.780 --> 19:15.060
The claim here is thrilling.

19:15.760 --> 19:17.700
It proposes that these early Neanderthals

19:17.700 --> 19:20.160
specifically selected, fractured,

19:20.320 --> 19:24.160
and arranged these stalagmites for their acoustic properties.

19:24.160 --> 19:27.620
That they build this massive 2.2 ton structure

19:27.620 --> 19:31.020
to create a coordinated group musical performance

19:31.020 --> 19:33.580
in a highly reverberant underground space.

19:33.580 --> 19:35.020
If this is true,

19:35.340 --> 19:39.020
it pushes the first attestation of organized musical practice

19:39.020 --> 19:44.900
back 140,000 years before those Swabian flutes we talked about.

19:45.140 --> 19:47.880
It rewrites the cognitive history of our entire genus.

19:48.220 --> 19:48.960
It really does.

19:48.960 --> 19:52.220
But we have to be incredibly precise here.

19:52.840 --> 19:54.240
They made a stone piano.

19:54.680 --> 19:55.880
Makes for a great headline.

19:56.420 --> 19:58.880
But it is way too broad of a claim for rigorous science.

19:59.900 --> 20:01.960
What Flyxion does so brilliantly in this paper

20:01.960 --> 20:05.160
is break this grand romantic hypothesis down

20:05.160 --> 20:08.480
into separable, individually testable versions.

20:08.900 --> 20:10.340
We have to look at a spectrum

20:10.340 --> 20:12.520
ranging from very strong claims

20:12.520 --> 20:13.900
to much weaker claims.

20:14.220 --> 20:15.620
Because if we lump them all together,

20:15.840 --> 20:16.960
we can't test any of them.

20:16.960 --> 20:18.700
Right, so let's parse those out.

20:19.400 --> 20:22.060
What is the absolute strongest version of this theory?

20:22.600 --> 20:25.380
The strongest version is the tuned lithophone.

20:26.040 --> 20:28.260
A lithophone being an instrument made of stone.

20:28.260 --> 20:31.040
Yes, like a xylophone, but rocks.

20:31.880 --> 20:34.260
This claim asserts that Neanderthals explicitly selected

20:34.820 --> 20:36.700
or deliberately chipped the fragments

20:36.700 --> 20:38.400
to produce a specific set

20:38.400 --> 20:40.960
of stable, perceptually distinct pitches.

20:41.260 --> 20:43.420
So they were literally tuning the rocks

20:43.420 --> 20:45.320
to create a prehistoric keyboard.

20:45.320 --> 20:48.460
Active, deliberate, pitch-specific tuning.

20:48.960 --> 20:50.300
Okay, what's the next step down?

20:51.020 --> 20:52.560
The second, slightly weaker version

20:52.560 --> 20:53.960
is acoustic selection.

20:54.780 --> 20:56.220
This means they chose the stones

20:56.220 --> 20:59.040
because they had a good ring to them when struck.

20:59.560 --> 21:02.860
So they preferred sonorous rocks over dull rocks,

21:02.940 --> 21:06.040
but they didn't necessarily try to chip them

21:06.040 --> 21:07.160
to tune them to each other.

21:07.480 --> 21:09.020
Right, they just wanted a collection

21:09.020 --> 21:10.140
of good-sounding stones.

21:10.140 --> 21:13.120
Okay, so a collection of resonant bells,

21:13.280 --> 21:15.160
but not necessarily a tuned scale.

21:15.520 --> 21:16.180
What's the third?

21:16.840 --> 21:19.780
The third version is the untuned percussion array.

21:20.580 --> 21:22.300
In this scenario, they gathered the stones

21:22.300 --> 21:24.060
and struck them purely for rhythm.

21:24.380 --> 21:25.080
Like a drum kit.

21:25.620 --> 21:26.000
Exactly.

21:26.380 --> 21:28.320
They used them as loud sound sources,

21:28.460 --> 21:30.380
but there was no cognitive requirement

21:30.380 --> 21:31.520
for stable pitch,

21:31.720 --> 21:33.640
no need for long acoustic decay,

21:33.880 --> 21:35.600
and no deliberate clustering

21:35.600 --> 21:36.900
of certain lengths of rock

21:36.900 --> 21:37.680
to get different notes.

21:37.680 --> 21:39.920
They just needed something hard to hit with a bone

21:39.920 --> 21:40.560
to keep a beat.

21:41.360 --> 21:42.380
And the weakest version?

21:43.000 --> 21:45.180
The fourth version is simply coordinated performance.

21:45.900 --> 21:47.800
This claims trips away the rocks entirely

21:47.800 --> 21:50.240
and just says the group used the built structures

21:50.240 --> 21:53.120
as a gathering place to make sound together,

21:53.620 --> 21:55.600
maybe chanting, clapping, or stomping.

21:55.720 --> 21:57.860
Specifically utilizing the reverberant echo

21:57.860 --> 21:58.840
of the deep cave,

21:59.160 --> 22:01.360
regardless of whether the stones themselves were struck.

22:01.580 --> 22:01.960
Exactly.

22:02.240 --> 22:04.340
So what does this all mean for us?

22:04.340 --> 22:06.600
It means that proving one version

22:06.600 --> 22:09.000
doesn't automatically prove the others.

22:09.460 --> 22:11.280
This is where so much pop science goes wrong.

22:11.960 --> 22:14.220
Striking a random rock with a bone to keep a rhythm,

22:14.780 --> 22:16.240
the untuned percussion array,

22:16.500 --> 22:19.580
is vastly different cognitively and technologically

22:19.580 --> 22:22.620
from actively chipping a massive piece of calcite

22:22.620 --> 22:24.860
to tune it to a specific frequency.

22:25.340 --> 22:26.060
Vastly different.

22:26.540 --> 22:28.420
And if we want to know if Neanderthals

22:28.420 --> 22:29.760
could actually tune rocks,

22:29.820 --> 22:31.000
we can't just guess.

22:31.000 --> 22:34.400
We have to understand the physics of how a rock makes a sound.

22:35.140 --> 22:37.840
Which brings us to the most mechanically intense

22:37.840 --> 22:39.720
mind-bending part of this paper.

22:40.240 --> 22:42.300
How does the stalagmite actually vibrate?

22:42.400 --> 22:42.580
Right.

22:42.980 --> 22:44.360
We need to talk about bending modes.

22:44.660 --> 22:44.900
Yes.

22:45.440 --> 22:46.580
To understand this,

22:46.740 --> 22:48.900
we have to look at the Euler-Bernoulli beam theory.

22:49.840 --> 22:50.500
To a physicist,

22:51.120 --> 22:52.680
a stalagmite isn't just a rock.

22:53.020 --> 22:56.300
It is essentially a tapered elastic rod.

22:56.500 --> 22:56.800
Okay.

22:56.800 --> 22:58.780
And when you strike an elastic rod,

22:58.980 --> 23:01.600
the lowest, most dominant frequencies it produces

23:01.600 --> 23:03.680
are what we call bending modes.

23:04.280 --> 23:07.900
So the rock physically flexes and bends?

23:08.140 --> 23:09.860
It oscillates back and forth, yes.

23:10.540 --> 23:10.840
Okay.

23:10.960 --> 23:13.540
I'm going to need a physical visualization here

23:13.540 --> 23:16.640
because when I hear bending modes and Euler-Bernoulli,

23:16.900 --> 23:18.400
my eyes just glaze over.

23:18.660 --> 23:19.620
That's completely fair.

23:19.620 --> 23:23.340
I think of taking a plastic ruler in middle school,

23:23.740 --> 23:25.520
holding it flat against the edge of a desk,

23:25.700 --> 23:27.560
and twanging the free end with my thumb.

23:27.860 --> 23:29.100
Is that what we're talking about?

23:29.300 --> 23:31.320
That is an absolutely perfect analogy.

23:32.000 --> 23:32.980
The frequency,

23:33.400 --> 23:35.620
the specific pitch of that plastic twang,

23:36.100 --> 23:38.320
depends on a very specific mathematical equation.

23:39.000 --> 23:40.820
In the Euler-Bernoulli theory,

23:41.140 --> 23:42.900
the pitch depends on the length of the ruler,

23:43.540 --> 23:44.520
the material stiffness,

23:44.960 --> 23:47.140
which engineers call Young's modulus,

23:47.140 --> 23:50.700
its density, and its cross-sectional area.

23:51.080 --> 23:51.140
Right.

23:51.620 --> 23:56.760
So if I have a long, thick, heavy, dense stalagmite,

23:56.900 --> 23:59.360
it vibrates slowly, making a deep pitch?

23:59.520 --> 23:59.880
Yes.

24:00.180 --> 24:03.060
And if I have a short, thin, light stalagmite,

24:03.160 --> 24:05.400
it vibrates incredibly fast, making a high pitch.

24:05.520 --> 24:06.580
That makes intuitive sense.

24:07.240 --> 24:07.680
It does.

24:08.200 --> 24:10.720
But here is the crucial variable that changes everything,

24:11.100 --> 24:13.960
and it is the key to the entire physical debate,

24:14.780 --> 24:16.060
the boundary conditions.

24:16.620 --> 24:17.440
Boundary conditions.

24:17.960 --> 24:20.240
What does that mean in the ruler analogy?

24:20.760 --> 24:21.980
Going back to your plastic ruler,

24:22.320 --> 24:24.860
if you press that ruler tight against the desk with your palm

24:24.860 --> 24:27.040
and twang the part hanging off the edge,

24:27.820 --> 24:31.100
that is what physicists call a clamped, free boundary condition.

24:31.800 --> 24:33.840
One end is immobilized, the other is free to move.

24:34.000 --> 24:34.400
Got it.

24:34.400 --> 24:38.780
But imagine you somehow suspended that exact same ruler in midair

24:38.780 --> 24:40.040
on two tiny threads,

24:40.340 --> 24:41.780
and you struck it with a pencil.

24:42.560 --> 24:44.460
That is a free, free boundary condition.

24:44.460 --> 24:48.200
The bar behaves entirely differently depending on how its ends are supported.

24:48.840 --> 24:52.440
Wait, so if the stalagmite is still attached to the cave floor,

24:52.620 --> 24:53.860
it's clamped free.

24:54.240 --> 24:56.640
The energy of the strike travels down the rock,

24:56.800 --> 24:59.160
hits the floor, and what, it just dies?

24:59.560 --> 25:00.460
Not just dies.

25:00.740 --> 25:01.760
It gets distorted.

25:02.560 --> 25:04.740
The floor acts as an infinite mass.

25:05.160 --> 25:07.900
The energy hits that boundary and reflects back erratically,

25:08.440 --> 25:09.800
squashing the natural waves.

25:09.800 --> 25:10.460
Oh, I see.

25:10.900 --> 25:14.140
The math of the pitch changes dramatically based on this.

25:14.620 --> 25:17.740
The text breaks down the specific decimal ratios of the overtones.

25:18.460 --> 25:20.940
For a bar that is completely free at both ends,

25:21.380 --> 25:22.940
the first four vibrational modes,

25:23.160 --> 25:26.420
that is the fundamental pitch and the higher ringing overtones above it,

25:26.960 --> 25:33.800
stand in a ratio of roughly 1 to 2.756 to 5.404 to 8.933.

25:33.800 --> 25:36.080
Okay, that's a lot of numbers.

25:36.300 --> 25:36.420
Right.

25:36.680 --> 25:40.900
But compare that to the exact same bar if it is clamped to the floor.

25:41.760 --> 25:47.260
The ratio has become 1 to 6.267 to 17.55.

25:47.780 --> 25:49.400
Those numbers are wildly different.

25:49.760 --> 25:52.020
But translate that into an auditory experience.

25:52.220 --> 25:54.840
What do those decimal ratios actually mean for my ear?

25:55.260 --> 25:57.700
Well, the paper says the overtones are inharmonic.

25:57.980 --> 25:59.120
Unpacking that is vital.

25:59.120 --> 26:02.660
When you pluck a guitar string or blow into a wooden flute,

26:02.900 --> 26:06.620
the overtones, the higher frequencies that ring out above the main note,

26:06.760 --> 26:07.560
are harmonic.

26:08.200 --> 26:11.860
They are neat, whole integer multiples of the fundamental note.

26:12.220 --> 26:12.500
Right.

26:12.580 --> 26:16.540
So if your bass note is 100 hertz, your overtones are 200, 300, 400.

26:17.120 --> 26:19.160
They stack perfectly on top of each other.

26:19.660 --> 26:22.780
This perfect stacking gives the note a clear, pleasing,

26:23.200 --> 26:25.420
instantly identifiable pitch to the human ear.

26:25.420 --> 26:30.360
But a rock, because of its physical stiffness and those messy boundary constraints,

26:30.800 --> 26:31.780
does not do that.

26:32.580 --> 26:35.460
Because of those messy decimal ratios we just listed,

26:36.020 --> 26:40.180
a struck stone produces a chaotic, inharmonic overtone structure.

26:40.560 --> 26:42.980
It does not follow a neat harmonic series.

26:43.400 --> 26:44.440
It sounds clangy.

26:44.980 --> 26:46.600
It sounds metallic or dissonant.

26:46.960 --> 26:51.040
It can be incredibly difficult for a human ear to assign a single distinct note to it

26:51.040 --> 26:54.580
because your brain is receiving a jumble of competing frequencies.

26:54.580 --> 26:58.120
The paper actually walks through a specific calculated example

26:58.120 --> 27:01.860
to show how extreme this boundary condition effect is on a stalagmite.

27:02.780 --> 27:05.000
Imagine a solid cylinder of calcite.

27:05.560 --> 27:10.800
It has a radius of 0.025 meters and a length of 0.40 meters.

27:11.160 --> 27:11.220
Okay.

27:11.600 --> 27:13.720
If that piece of calcite is totally free,

27:13.840 --> 27:17.260
meaning a Neanderthal broke it off entirely and rested it lightly on two points,

27:17.740 --> 27:20.760
its fundamental pitch is roughly 1,490 hertz.

27:20.960 --> 27:23.920
That is a high, piercing bell-like ring.

27:23.920 --> 27:25.520
Yeah, that's a very clear tone.

27:26.260 --> 27:31.100
But if that exact same piece of calcite is still attached to the floor, clamped free,

27:31.440 --> 27:35.780
the fundamental pitch plummets all the way down to about 235 hertz.

27:36.140 --> 27:40.460
It drops from a high ring to a deep, low, muddy thud.

27:40.840 --> 27:43.200
And this is where Fleishen offers a very sharp,

27:43.600 --> 27:48.620
almost surgical critique of the original researchers who proposed the stone piano hypothesis.

27:48.860 --> 27:49.920
Oh, this part is great.

27:49.920 --> 27:55.420
Apparently, the original paper ran this exact Euler-Bernoulli calculation for a clamped stalagmite

27:55.420 --> 27:59.140
and reported a fundamental pitch value of around 320 hertz.

27:59.840 --> 28:03.160
But Fleishen runs the math and gets 235 hertz.

28:03.420 --> 28:03.880
Exactly.

28:04.460 --> 28:06.920
And Fleishen rightly demands that this discrepancy be recomputed.

28:07.860 --> 28:09.860
It is a brilliant moment of scientific rigor.

28:09.860 --> 28:15.260
It highlights that if you are going to do acoustic archaeology and claim a species-invented instruments

28:15.260 --> 28:21.060
176,000 years ago, your foundational math has to be absolutely ironclad.

28:21.460 --> 28:26.780
You cannot base a massive historical claim on a flawed application of a physics equation.

28:27.320 --> 28:29.100
Here's where it gets really interesting, though.

28:29.200 --> 28:33.920
Let's move past the math error and assume the Neanderthals did break the stalagmites off.

28:34.260 --> 28:37.100
They now have a free, free bar of stone lying on the ground.

28:37.100 --> 28:39.400
Okay, so they have the raw material.

28:39.840 --> 28:40.580
How do they tune it?

28:41.120 --> 28:43.320
They don't know the Euler-Bernoulli equation.

28:44.200 --> 28:46.940
The text points out a fascinating physical reality.

28:47.520 --> 28:50.680
Frequency scales with the inverse square of the length.

28:51.080 --> 28:51.360
Yes.

28:51.860 --> 28:57.580
And that specific mathematical relationship is incredibly powerful when translated into physical practice.

28:57.580 --> 29:08.580
It means that if you have a stalagmite fragment, and you use a hand axe to knock a small chip off the end, reducing its overall length by just 10%, the pitch of that rock doesn't just go up 10%.

29:09.140 --> 29:13.580
The inverse square law dictates that the pitch jumps up by roughly 23%.

29:14.220 --> 29:18.920
It's a massive, highly audible leap in pitch for a very small physical change.

29:19.320 --> 29:26.580
This proves mathematically that knocking a small chip off the end of a rock is a highly effective, albeit slightly coarse, tuning method.

29:26.580 --> 29:32.900
You don't need a physics degree to realize that making the rock just a tiny bit shorter makes the pitch shoot up rapidly.

29:33.440 --> 29:35.820
It's an easily observable, repeatable phenomenon.

29:36.320 --> 29:41.040
But knowing that chipping changes the pitch brings us to a huge, practical, on-the-ground problem.

29:41.640 --> 29:47.660
We've established that hitting a rock to shorten it raises its pitch, but a Neanderthal doesn't have a digital tuner pedal.

29:47.900 --> 29:48.860
They don't have a pitch pipe.

29:49.040 --> 29:51.280
They don't have an abstract understanding of acoustic theory.

29:51.280 --> 29:58.500
How could they actually practically tune a multi-ton lithophone deep in a dark, damp cave?

29:59.020 --> 30:00.520
That is the operational hurdle.

30:01.280 --> 30:09.740
The original hypothesis proposed four potential tuning methods, and the crucial thread connecting all of them is that they rely solely on perceptual feedback.

30:10.140 --> 30:11.940
They are embodied practices.

30:11.940 --> 30:12.380
Right.

30:13.180 --> 30:14.720
They don't require abstract theory.

30:15.240 --> 30:18.100
They require a good ear and physical experimentation.

30:18.940 --> 30:21.020
The first method is progressive fracture.

30:21.680 --> 30:24.360
Which is essentially brute force trial and error.

30:24.900 --> 30:25.460
You have a rock.

30:26.100 --> 30:29.380
You chip a piece off the end, you strike it, and you listen.

30:30.100 --> 30:33.080
You chip another piece off, strike it, listen again.

30:33.580 --> 30:36.000
And keep going until the pitch sounds right to your ear.

30:36.000 --> 30:39.060
It's simple, but it feels incredibly risky.

30:39.640 --> 30:46.560
Because of that inverse square law, if you hit it slightly too hard and break off too big a chunk, the pitch shoots up way past what you wanted.

30:46.880 --> 30:48.180
And you can't glue the rock back on.

30:48.500 --> 30:48.900
Exactly.

30:49.380 --> 30:52.720
The piece is ruined, and you have to start over with a brand new stalagmite.

30:53.360 --> 30:54.540
The failure rate would be high.

30:55.160 --> 30:57.080
That leads to the second proposed method.

30:57.940 --> 30:59.300
Comparative beat matching.

30:59.820 --> 31:00.640
This one is cool.

31:01.380 --> 31:03.600
It relies on a universal acoustic phenomenon.

31:03.600 --> 31:13.180
If you strike a fragment you are trying to tune, and then you quickly strike a reverence stone that is already at the pitch you want, the two sound waves interact in the air.

31:13.980 --> 31:16.900
If they are slightly out of tune, their frequencies clash.

31:17.300 --> 31:24.840
Yes, and they create an audible, rhythmic pulsing sound, a wub-wub-wub effect, which physicists call acoustic beating.

31:25.480 --> 31:31.120
You keep shortening your fragment until that pulsing slows down and eventually stops completely.

31:31.120 --> 31:35.620
When the wub-wub disappears, the two stones are perfectly mathematically in tune.

31:36.280 --> 31:36.820
I love that.

31:37.260 --> 31:42.100
It uses the literal physics of sound waves as a natural auditory tuning fork.

31:42.540 --> 31:43.800
It's an elegant solution.

31:44.960 --> 31:50.020
But sound doesn't just happen in a vacuum, especially not 336 meters underground.

31:50.660 --> 31:52.460
It interacts with the space around it.

31:52.660 --> 31:57.420
I'm assuming they had to use the actual architecture of the cave to help them figure out what sounded good.

31:57.420 --> 32:01.400
You're touching exactly on the third method, cavity amplification.

32:01.800 --> 32:03.800
Which is a physical spatial hack.

32:04.040 --> 32:04.300
Right.

32:04.940 --> 32:12.100
You hold the rock you are testing over a natural hollow or depression in the cave wall, or even just cup your hands tightly behind the stone.

32:12.800 --> 32:17.080
The cavity acts as a Helmholtz resonator, reinforcing specific frequencies.

32:17.420 --> 32:24.240
If the rock's frequency matches the resonant frequency of that specific cavity, the sound suddenly gets much louder and richer.

32:24.620 --> 32:25.520
It blooms.

32:25.520 --> 32:29.420
The cave wall itself helps you isolate the right pitch.

32:30.140 --> 32:31.560
It's like singing in the shower.

32:32.260 --> 32:35.480
Your voice suddenly finds that one note that makes the whole bathroom ring.

32:35.820 --> 32:36.920
Exactly like that.

32:37.620 --> 32:39.660
And the fourth original method.

32:40.080 --> 32:42.900
The fourth is assembly-guided sympathetic tuning.

32:44.000 --> 32:46.660
This takes the tuning process into the structure itself.

32:47.280 --> 32:49.600
Let's say you've already built half of your stone ring.

32:49.820 --> 32:50.040
Okay.

32:50.040 --> 32:53.800
You test a new, unplaced stone by hitting it near the constructed ring.

32:54.300 --> 33:03.100
If the new rock is perfectly in tune with the others, the acoustic energy traveling through the air will cause the already placed rocks to start vibrating sympathetically.

33:03.640 --> 33:05.680
The structure itself confirms the tuning?

33:05.940 --> 33:06.220
Right.

33:06.220 --> 33:10.460
Those four methods are brilliant because they rely on natural feedback loops.

33:10.680 --> 33:14.780
But Flyxion, the author of our text, isn't satisfied with just those four.

33:15.460 --> 33:19.440
Flyxion brings two brand new, highly original proposals to the table.

33:19.680 --> 33:23.660
And they are fascinating precisely because they can be rigorously tested in a lab.

33:23.660 --> 33:26.660
They push the boundaries of what we consider paleolithic engineering.

33:27.460 --> 33:27.920
They really do.

33:28.160 --> 33:33.040
The first new proposal Flyxion introduces is suspension and rotation on cords.

33:33.500 --> 33:37.780
Now, remember when we talked about the free-free bar suspending the ruler on two threads?

33:38.120 --> 33:38.400
Yes.

33:38.740 --> 33:47.000
The math dictates that a free ringing bar has acoustic notes, specific spots along its length where the material naturally does not vibrate as much when struck.

33:47.000 --> 33:54.960
Right. The Euler-Brunoulli equations say these nodes are located at exactly 0.224 times the length of the rock from each end.

33:55.580 --> 33:59.960
If you hold or suspend the rock at exactly those node points, you don't deaden the vibration.

34:00.580 --> 34:04.480
You allow the antinodes, the middle and the very ends, to flex maximally.

34:04.960 --> 34:07.800
You get the longest, clearest, most sustained ring possible.

34:08.100 --> 34:09.800
If you grab it in the middle, it just thuds.

34:10.180 --> 34:14.460
But obviously, a Neanderthal isn't measuring 0.224 with a prehistoric tape measure.

34:14.460 --> 34:17.420
So, Flyxion suggests an organic method.

34:17.840 --> 34:23.920
They tied a cord, maybe made of animal sinew or braided plant fiber, around the rock to suspend it.

34:24.280 --> 34:31.000
You just slide the knot up and down the rock, repeatedly striking it until you find the exact spot where the rock rings the longest and loudest.

34:31.640 --> 34:33.660
You don't need to know the math to find the node.

34:34.100 --> 34:37.220
Your ears and the physical decay of the sound find it for you.

34:37.620 --> 34:39.480
You are minimizing the damping by feel.

34:39.480 --> 34:45.700
And once that stone is suspended on a cord, Flyxion introduces a truly wild acoustic concept.

34:46.540 --> 34:47.960
The aerodynamic whistle.

34:48.700 --> 34:50.460
I have to admit, this is where I struggled.

34:50.620 --> 34:52.080
I'm trying to visualize this.

34:52.660 --> 34:53.480
It's quite the image.

34:53.800 --> 35:00.000
The author suggests they could have whirled a worked stone point like a heavy flint arrowhead on the end of a long cord.

35:00.460 --> 35:06.080
When an object moves rapidly through the air, it sheds vortices in its wake, creating sound.

35:06.400 --> 35:06.680
Right.

35:06.680 --> 35:14.800
And there is a mathematical constant for predicting this pitch called the Strohal number, which is roughly 0.2 for many aerodynamic shapes.

35:15.300 --> 35:25.540
So if you whirl a stone point that is 0.02 meters wide at a speed of 10 meters per second, the math says it produces a frequency of about 100 hertz.

35:26.180 --> 35:28.200
It makes a low continuous humming tone.

35:28.740 --> 35:30.720
It's essentially a primitive bull roar.

35:31.000 --> 35:33.560
It creates a stable continuous drone.

35:33.880 --> 35:34.120
Yes.

35:34.120 --> 35:36.580
But here is my pushback.

35:37.360 --> 35:40.880
I have a heavy calcite stalagmite lying on the floor that I want to tune.

35:41.580 --> 35:45.660
And I have my friend standing next to me, swinging a rock on a string over their head to make a hum.

35:46.340 --> 35:51.680
How does the hum of a swinging rock help me chip a stationary stalagmite to the right length?

35:52.220 --> 35:53.660
It does seem disconnected at first.

35:54.160 --> 35:59.480
It sounds like we are combining two completely different physical mechanisms that don't relate to each other.

35:59.480 --> 36:03.040
It is a leap, but it addresses a fundamental problem in music.

36:03.820 --> 36:04.640
Absolute pitch.

36:05.320 --> 36:09.760
How do you remember what note you are trying to tune to day after day in the dark?

36:10.300 --> 36:12.760
Very few humans have perfect pitch memory.

36:13.040 --> 36:13.380
Exactly.

36:13.380 --> 36:24.980
But if you know that whirling a specific, easily portable stone point on a specific length of cord always produces exactly 100 hertz, you have invented a standardized reference pitch.

36:25.520 --> 36:26.040
Ah.

36:26.620 --> 36:28.480
The bull roar gives you the baseline drone.

36:29.040 --> 36:35.320
You then use the comparative beat matching, the wub-wub method, to tune your stalagmite to the drone of the swinging stone.

36:35.320 --> 36:39.060
You've created an acoustic standard that can be carried in a pouch.

36:39.280 --> 36:39.440
Yes.

36:40.080 --> 36:44.380
Okay, when you explain it as a prehistoric tuning fork, that makes incredible sense.

36:44.860 --> 36:45.780
It's a reference drone.

36:46.440 --> 36:51.460
That leads us to Flyxion's second new proposal, which might be my favorite concept in the entire paper.

36:52.100 --> 36:54.900
Sorting by partials against a tunable membrane.

36:55.240 --> 37:01.600
This is an incredibly elegant, visually stunning idea for managing the chaotic nature of stone acoustics.

37:01.600 --> 37:06.600
Let's say you have an animal hide stretched tightly over a wooden frame, essentially a primitive drum.

37:07.260 --> 37:18.940
That membrane has its own inharmonic modes, its own messy ratios of vibration based on tension and diameter, which the paper notes roughly as 1 to 1.59 to 2.14, and so on.

37:18.940 --> 37:34.040
If you strike a stone and hold it very close to that stretched hide, and if the stone's chaotic overtones happen to perfectly align with the hide's messy overtones, the sound waves will cause the hide to vibrate sympathetically without you ever touching it.

37:34.280 --> 37:36.420
Which is pretty cool on its own.

37:36.520 --> 37:39.200
But I want you to picture this, because the implications are profound.

37:39.640 --> 37:45.880
It's like taking a drum, laying it flat, and sprinkling a handful of fine sand or tiny seeds onto the drum head.

37:46.000 --> 37:46.180
Right.

37:46.180 --> 37:49.380
Then, you strike your stalagmite and hold it near the drum.

37:49.980 --> 38:02.820
If you hit the exact right resonant frequency, the acoustic energy transfers into the hide, and the sand suddenly starts bouncing and dancing, organizing itself into complex geometric shapes called clodney patterns.

38:02.920 --> 38:05.860
It's a visual, tactile equalizer.

38:06.120 --> 38:10.420
It translates invisible acoustic energy into visible kinetic energy.

38:10.840 --> 38:12.720
Think about how profound that is.

38:12.720 --> 38:29.360
Tens of thousands of years before the invention of the oscilloscope, or digital audio analysis or frequency spectrum graphs, early hominids could theoretically have been using stretched animal skins to make the invisible spectrum of sound physically visible.

38:29.880 --> 38:37.140
You could test hundreds of rocks against this one drum, and literally sort them into piles based on which ones make the sand jump.

38:37.140 --> 38:42.300
You are sorting rocks by their complex overtone structures without ever needing a computer.

38:42.940 --> 38:46.260
It is prehistoric visual audio analysis.

38:46.640 --> 38:49.620
It is a beautiful, ingenious concept.

38:49.620 --> 38:55.100
It paints a picture of highly sophisticated, sensory-integrated paleolithic engineering.

38:55.600 --> 38:58.780
But, and here is where the paper sharply pivots.

38:59.520 --> 39:00.240
Theories are great.

39:01.040 --> 39:03.400
Speculative physical models are fun to talk about.

39:03.820 --> 39:08.620
But this text is ultimately a scientific document, and it demands rigorous testing.

39:08.620 --> 39:15.240
You cannot publish a theory about prehistoric audio visualizers without providing a roadmap to prove it.

39:15.680 --> 39:20.680
We have to convert these brilliant speculations into actual, falsifiable science.

39:20.920 --> 39:26.100
Which brings us perfectly to the next major phase of the paper, testing the untestable.

39:27.200 --> 39:29.560
Flyxion doesn't just drop these ideas and walk away.

39:29.980 --> 39:33.600
The author outlines seven specific experimental paradigms.

39:33.600 --> 39:39.500
And rather than just marching through them alphabetically, let's group them by what they are actually trying to prove.

39:40.420 --> 39:43.460
As we walk through these, I want you to put on your peer reviewer hat.

39:44.040 --> 39:46.840
Think like a skeptical scientist trying to poke holes in a theory.

39:47.400 --> 39:50.380
Because for each test, Flyxion lists the predicted outcome.

39:50.700 --> 39:55.160
But more importantly, Flyxion lists the outcome that would definitively kill the hypothesis.

39:55.740 --> 39:58.120
That is the essence of the scientific method.

39:58.480 --> 40:01.580
A theory is only as good as its falsification criteria.

40:01.580 --> 40:05.340
If a theory cannot be disproven, it isn't science.

40:05.840 --> 40:05.940
Right.

40:06.180 --> 40:14.220
So if you're Flyxion, the absolute first thing you have to prove is that this material, calcite stalagmites, isn't just dead, acoustically useless rock.

40:14.540 --> 40:17.040
You have to test the material and the human ear.

40:17.580 --> 40:18.640
Paradigms A and B.

40:19.080 --> 40:24.260
You take modern stalagmite analogs, you cut them into varying lengths in a lab, and you physically strike them.

40:24.400 --> 40:28.140
The prediction is that they produce clear, sustained, pitched tones.

40:28.500 --> 40:29.220
But what if they don't?

40:29.440 --> 40:31.400
What if a stalagmite is just too dense?

40:31.580 --> 40:36.720
Or two internally fractured from natural formation, and hitting it just produces a dull thud?

40:37.400 --> 40:41.200
If it just thuds, then the tuned lithophone theory dies right there on the lab table.

40:41.560 --> 40:43.000
You cannot tune a thud.

40:43.420 --> 40:44.680
There is no pitch to manipulate.

40:45.520 --> 40:50.880
If that foundational material test fails, we are left only with the untuned percussion theory.

40:51.760 --> 40:54.420
They were just hitting rocks for a blunt rhythm, not for melody.

40:54.420 --> 40:58.200
And assuming it does ring, you then have to test human perception.

40:58.560 --> 41:08.340
You take the high-quality audio recordings of those ringing rocks from the lab, and you play them to a large group of naive, modern human listeners, people with no specialized musical training.

41:08.340 --> 41:13.480
The prediction is that listeners will generally agree on what the dominant pitch is.

41:13.480 --> 41:18.440
But as we discussed with the Euler-Bernoulli math, the overtones of calcite are chaotic.

41:19.100 --> 41:24.620
What if they are so inharmonic that ten different humans hear ten completely different notes?

41:24.620 --> 41:28.300
Then the hypothesis is in deep, terminal trouble.

41:29.260 --> 41:36.320
If a modern human ear, aided by a clean recording, cannot extract a stable pitch from the acoustic mess of a stalagmite,

41:36.860 --> 41:43.500
then it is highly unlikely Neanderthals could have accurately tuned them by ear in a noisy cave.

41:43.720 --> 41:47.240
The entire perceptual feedback loop required for tuning breaks down.

41:47.560 --> 41:47.800
Right.

41:47.800 --> 41:54.220
Okay, so let's assume the rock rings, and humans can agree on the pitch.

41:54.860 --> 42:01.120
The next phase of testing has to prove that a Neanderthal could actually execute these tuning methods without modern tools.

42:01.400 --> 42:02.320
You have to test the hacks.

42:02.880 --> 42:04.200
Paradigms C, D, and E.

42:04.440 --> 42:08.880
You give untrained people modern rock fragments, a hand axe, and a reference tone.

42:09.140 --> 42:14.020
Ask them to use the progressive fracture method to chip the rock until the pitch matches.

42:14.020 --> 42:17.660
Can they actually do it, or do they shatter the rock every time?

42:18.320 --> 42:21.320
You also have to test the membrane sorting in a controlled environment.

42:22.140 --> 42:29.860
Set up a vibrating animal hide, strike the stones, sort the rocks based on which ones make the membrane resonate, or the sand jump,

42:30.120 --> 42:34.340
and then run those sorted rocks through modern digital spectral measurement equipment.

42:34.900 --> 42:39.420
Does the primitive drum sorting actually correlate with the real objective acoustic data?

42:39.420 --> 42:45.120
If the rocks in the resonating pile actually have completely different overtone structures, according to the computer,

42:45.920 --> 42:49.060
then the membrane theory is discarded as a viable tuning method.

42:49.580 --> 42:52.240
It might look cool, but it isn't mathematically accurate.

42:52.520 --> 42:52.860
Exactly.

42:52.860 --> 42:54.920
And you have to test the whirling stones.

42:55.240 --> 42:55.940
Go out to a field.

42:56.220 --> 43:00.660
Tie a flint point to a cord at the exact 0.224 node point.

43:01.240 --> 43:02.840
Whirl it at 10 meters per second.

43:03.560 --> 43:07.640
Does it actually create a stable, measurable 100 hertz tone?

43:08.280 --> 43:12.600
Or is the aerodynamic reality much messier than the Strauhau number suggests?

43:13.060 --> 43:17.120
Is it just a chaotic, whooshing sound that provides no clear reference pitch at all?

43:17.380 --> 43:21.320
You have to prove the mechanics in the present before you can project them into the past.

43:21.320 --> 43:25.420
But even if all those modern mechanical tests succeed perfectly,

43:25.940 --> 43:30.200
even if we prove that tuning stalagmites is entirely physically feasible,

43:30.740 --> 43:34.940
we still haven't proven that the Neanderthals at Brinequal actually did it.

43:35.380 --> 43:38.540
That brings us to the final and most critical set of paradigms.

43:39.060 --> 43:41.300
Testing the physical evidence of the cave itself.

43:42.420 --> 43:43.860
Paradigms F and G.

43:44.300 --> 43:48.820
This is where we look at the actual 176,000-year-old crime scene.

43:48.820 --> 43:53.560
Flyxion proposes looking at chamber acoustics and surface wear.

43:54.600 --> 43:58.520
First, does that specific chamber, 336 meters deep,

43:59.080 --> 44:02.740
actually reverberate long enough to support overlapping musical tones?

44:02.980 --> 44:06.320
If the echo dies instantly, a stone piano makes no sense.

44:06.940 --> 44:10.060
But more importantly, are there percussion wear marks on the stones?

44:10.060 --> 44:13.960
If you are hitting a rock repeatedly with a bone hammer for years,

44:14.280 --> 44:17.040
you're going to leave microscopic impact craters.

44:17.760 --> 44:20.240
Are those craters located at the exact antinodes,

44:20.560 --> 44:23.700
the mathematically optimal places to strike a bar to make it ring?

44:24.160 --> 44:27.400
If you find concentrated wear marks exactly at the antinodes,

44:27.520 --> 44:28.520
you have your smoking gun.

44:28.740 --> 44:31.120
You have proven intentional acoustic use.

44:31.460 --> 44:33.400
But if we connect this to the bigger picture,

44:33.940 --> 44:37.400
the absence of wear marks isn't necessarily a deal-breaker, is it?

44:37.400 --> 44:40.860
I mean, 176,000 years is a long time.

44:41.480 --> 44:43.140
That is a highly critical nuance,

44:43.520 --> 44:46.560
and it's why archaeology is so incredibly difficult.

44:47.440 --> 44:49.300
Flyxion explicitly reminds the reader

44:49.300 --> 44:51.720
that an absence of wear marks only matters

44:51.720 --> 44:53.940
if the preservation conditions in the cave

44:53.940 --> 44:56.580
would have allowed those microscopic marks to survive.

44:56.940 --> 44:57.840
Remember the context.

44:58.040 --> 45:00.240
These structures were heavily exposed to fire.

45:00.240 --> 45:03.240
The heat could have spalled off the outer layer of the rock.

45:03.840 --> 45:08.040
They sat in a damp, geologically active cave for 176 millennia,

45:08.580 --> 45:11.200
constantly collecting new mineral deposits from dripping water.

45:11.480 --> 45:15.180
They may have suffered later disturbances from animal activity or kivens.

45:15.720 --> 45:18.080
So if you put the stones under a microscope

45:18.080 --> 45:20.400
and you don't find wear marks,

45:20.780 --> 45:22.500
you have to ask a terrifying question.

45:22.980 --> 45:24.480
Is it because they were never struck?

45:24.480 --> 45:30.560
Or is it because 170 millennia of fire, water, and time

45:30.560 --> 45:32.880
simply erased the evidence?

45:33.720 --> 45:37.280
It's forensic science on an impossible mind-bending timeline.

45:38.080 --> 45:40.500
But there is one more test Flyxion proposes,

45:40.840 --> 45:42.420
and it might be the cleverest one of all.

45:42.720 --> 45:44.980
It bypasses the wear marks entirely.

45:45.300 --> 45:47.560
It's the morphometric null model.

45:47.980 --> 45:48.320
Yes.

45:48.640 --> 45:50.840
I want to highlight this as the most crucial,

45:51.040 --> 45:53.920
direct, mathematical test of the entire paper.

45:53.920 --> 45:56.780
This is how you separate intention from coincidence.

45:57.840 --> 45:58.040
Hold on.

45:58.620 --> 46:00.300
Before we dive in how it works,

46:01.220 --> 46:03.580
morphometric null models is a great way

46:03.580 --> 46:05.360
to lose our audience in academic jargon.

46:05.840 --> 46:06.320
Fair point.

46:07.040 --> 46:08.520
In plain English, we're asking,

46:09.220 --> 46:10.760
what if they just grabbed rocks

46:10.760 --> 46:12.660
that were all roughly the same size

46:12.660 --> 46:15.200
because those rocks fit nicely in their hands,

46:15.360 --> 46:17.500
and it has absolutely nothing to do with music?

46:17.820 --> 46:18.300
Precisely.

46:18.480 --> 46:19.980
That is the null model.

46:20.420 --> 46:22.920
The null model is the hypothesis of random chance

46:22.920 --> 46:24.940
or purely structural utility.

46:25.340 --> 46:28.080
You take the actual physical Bruniquel fragments.

46:28.580 --> 46:31.900
The paper notes their mean length is roughly 0.28 meters.

46:32.300 --> 46:33.620
You take all of their dimensions,

46:34.400 --> 46:35.420
length, width, density,

46:35.540 --> 46:37.480
and you feed them into a computer simulation

46:37.480 --> 46:39.220
to predict their acoustic responses

46:39.220 --> 46:40.500
using Euler-Bernoulli.

46:40.500 --> 46:43.880
Then you compare those predicted acoustics

46:43.880 --> 46:45.380
against a simulated null model.

46:45.540 --> 46:46.800
What would the acoustics look like

46:46.800 --> 46:49.220
if rocks simply fell from the cave ceiling,

46:49.380 --> 46:51.200
fractured naturally upon hitting the floor,

46:51.420 --> 46:53.140
and were gathered by Neanderthals

46:53.140 --> 46:55.500
purely to build a structurally stable wall

46:55.500 --> 46:57.280
with zero thought given to sound?

46:57.960 --> 47:00.240
If the acoustic profile of the real,

47:00.400 --> 47:02.020
carefully arranged Bruniquel rocks

47:02.020 --> 47:03.760
is completely indistinguishable

47:03.760 --> 47:06.260
from the random structural null model,

47:06.900 --> 47:09.400
then the acoustic selection hypothesis is unproven.

47:09.400 --> 47:10.320
It's dead in the water.

47:11.520 --> 47:14.060
You cannot claim they meticulously pick the rocks

47:14.060 --> 47:15.420
for their beautiful sound

47:15.420 --> 47:18.140
if random chants and basic masonry

47:18.140 --> 47:20.140
produce the exact same acoustic results.

47:20.320 --> 47:20.640
Exactly.

47:21.800 --> 47:23.820
The acoustic signature of the built rings

47:23.820 --> 47:26.740
has to be statistically undeniably anomalous

47:26.740 --> 47:27.980
compared to the background rubble.

47:28.540 --> 47:31.480
It has to show clear, non-random clustering of pitches.

47:32.100 --> 47:34.120
If it doesn't, it's just a wall.

47:34.840 --> 47:35.860
Okay, so let's zoom out.

47:35.860 --> 47:38.120
Let's say Flyxion runs all these tests,

47:38.300 --> 47:40.900
and against all odds, they actually succeed.

47:41.700 --> 47:43.160
The modern stalagmites ring clearly.

47:43.780 --> 47:46.660
The naive listeners can easily identify the pitch.

47:47.100 --> 47:49.400
The membrane sorting works perfectly in the lab,

47:49.660 --> 47:52.500
and the null models show a statistically impossible,

47:52.960 --> 47:55.940
deliberate acoustic clustering of the Bruniquel stones.

47:56.360 --> 47:57.900
The hypothesis survives the gauntlet.

47:58.240 --> 48:01.340
We still have a massive evolutionary puzzle to solve.

48:01.340 --> 48:04.780
How do you go from a species that supposedly just grunts

48:04.780 --> 48:06.200
and hits things with sticks

48:06.200 --> 48:08.900
to an organized society building a massive,

48:09.060 --> 48:10.920
detached, mathematically coherent,

48:11.100 --> 48:12.340
lithophone deep underground?

48:12.700 --> 48:13.900
You don't just wake up one morning

48:13.900 --> 48:16.060
and invent a 2.2 ton stone piano.

48:16.280 --> 48:17.180
You certainly don't.

48:17.740 --> 48:19.580
It requires an evolutionary pathway.

48:20.180 --> 48:23.040
This takes us to the final section of Flyxion's paper.

48:23.720 --> 48:24.880
The developmental sequence

48:24.880 --> 48:27.100
and the absolute limits of inference.

48:27.100 --> 48:30.280
Flyxion proposes a highly specific,

48:30.740 --> 48:32.160
logical developmental sequence

48:32.160 --> 48:34.340
to explain how this could have evolved.

48:34.860 --> 48:37.540
But Flyxion insists, vigorously,

48:37.820 --> 48:39.420
that we must treat this sequence

48:39.420 --> 48:42.280
as a set of completely separable conjectures.

48:43.280 --> 48:46.100
Because if we don't evaluate each step independently,

48:46.340 --> 48:48.420
we risk what the author calls overreach.

48:48.560 --> 48:49.000
Overreach.

48:49.240 --> 48:50.880
Which in science is when a researcher

48:50.880 --> 48:53.620
proves one tiny, specific thing in a lab

48:53.620 --> 48:55.500
and immediately uses it to claim

48:55.500 --> 48:57.980
they've solved the entire macro mystery of the universe.

48:58.560 --> 48:58.880
Precisely.

48:59.060 --> 49:00.340
You prove a rock can ring

49:00.340 --> 49:03.180
and suddenly you're claiming Neanderthals had symphonies.

49:03.340 --> 49:04.440
You have to take it step by step.

49:05.680 --> 49:09.360
The proposed sequence is four distinct evolutionary steps.

49:09.700 --> 49:10.080
Step one.

49:11.280 --> 49:13.440
Swinging cords and whirling stones,

49:13.620 --> 49:14.800
those aerodynamic whistles,

49:15.360 --> 49:17.700
built an embodied physical control

49:17.700 --> 49:18.720
of rhythm and rotation.

49:19.220 --> 49:21.220
It trained their neuromuscular systems.

49:21.220 --> 49:22.620
Step two.

49:23.160 --> 49:24.920
While engaged in this rhythmic play,

49:25.020 --> 49:27.600
they organically discovered comparative beat calibration.

49:28.240 --> 49:29.900
They noticed the wub-wub sound

49:29.900 --> 49:31.200
when striking two different rocks

49:31.200 --> 49:33.540
and learned how to manipulate it by chipping the stone.

49:34.200 --> 49:34.840
Step three.

49:35.340 --> 49:38.040
This highly specialized, embodied acoustic knowledge

49:38.040 --> 49:40.260
was transmitted culturally across generations.

49:40.920 --> 49:43.440
Not by writing down Euler-Brunoulli equations,

49:43.700 --> 49:45.160
but by physical demonstration.

49:45.920 --> 49:46.620
An elder saying,

49:46.740 --> 49:48.560
watch me chip this rock until it sounds right.

49:49.180 --> 49:49.980
And then you do it.

49:49.980 --> 49:51.560
And then the final leap.

49:52.140 --> 49:52.480
Step four.

49:53.220 --> 49:54.860
A technological transition occurred.

49:55.560 --> 49:57.480
They moved from simply striking stalagmites

49:57.480 --> 49:59.120
that were still attached to the cave floor,

49:59.540 --> 50:01.400
which we know produces a muddy thud,

50:01.880 --> 50:03.760
to deliberately breaking them off,

50:04.060 --> 50:04.800
transporting them,

50:05.080 --> 50:07.360
and building constructed detached arrays

50:07.360 --> 50:09.640
specifically to manipulate the boundary conditions

50:09.640 --> 50:11.620
and maximize the acoustic potential.

50:12.120 --> 50:14.080
It is a beautiful logical progression.

50:14.400 --> 50:15.900
It makes perfect evolutionary sense.

50:15.900 --> 50:17.900
But here is where we have to introduce

50:17.900 --> 50:20.420
the ultimate buzzkill of this entire deep dive.

50:21.100 --> 50:23.280
It is perhaps the most important scientific concept

50:23.280 --> 50:24.200
in the entire paper,

50:24.380 --> 50:25.740
and it is a word everyone should add

50:25.740 --> 50:27.260
to their critical thinking vocabulary.

50:28.600 --> 50:29.120
Equifinality.

50:30.320 --> 50:30.840
Equifinality.

50:31.440 --> 50:32.240
I love this concept.

50:32.780 --> 50:35.360
It is the principle that completely different causes,

50:35.640 --> 50:37.160
completely different historical events,

50:37.240 --> 50:39.700
can produce the exact same final physical result.

50:40.020 --> 50:40.280
Right.

50:40.780 --> 50:42.500
Let's apply it directly to Brunickel.

50:42.500 --> 50:44.920
We look at this massive ring of stones.

50:45.700 --> 50:47.020
We marvel at how all the pieces

50:47.020 --> 50:49.300
are roughly 0.28 meters long.

50:49.980 --> 50:51.500
We run our acoustic simulations,

50:51.680 --> 50:52.020
and we think,

50:52.220 --> 50:53.460
ah, look at the resonance.

50:54.000 --> 50:56.200
They carefully selected these specific lengths

50:56.200 --> 50:58.880
to create a unified, reverberant harmony.

50:59.540 --> 51:02.120
But Equifinality taps us on the shoulder and says,

51:02.640 --> 51:04.880
what if 0.28 meters is just the most

51:04.880 --> 51:06.360
ergonomically comfortable size

51:06.360 --> 51:09.140
for a Neanderthal to carry 336 meters in the dark?

51:09.800 --> 51:11.660
What if a 0.28 meter stone

51:11.660 --> 51:13.940
creates the most structurally interlocking,

51:14.140 --> 51:16.580
stable wall to keep a fire from spreading?

51:17.220 --> 51:18.860
A ring of similar-length stones

51:18.860 --> 51:20.720
might exist entirely for structural,

51:20.960 --> 51:22.400
ergonomic, or thermal reasons,

51:22.760 --> 51:25.260
leaving no intentional acoustic signature whatsoever.

51:25.840 --> 51:27.400
The fact that it happens to sound good

51:27.400 --> 51:27.940
when you hit it

51:27.940 --> 51:29.900
might be a complete physical coincidence.

51:30.360 --> 51:31.700
This is the golden rule of the text,

51:31.800 --> 51:34.580
and truly the golden rule of all archaeology.

51:35.400 --> 51:36.800
Demonstrating that a tuning method

51:36.800 --> 51:38.180
works in a modern lab,

51:38.840 --> 51:40.540
proving its physical feasibility,

51:40.540 --> 51:42.600
does not prove that Neanderthals

51:42.600 --> 51:44.160
actually used it in the past.

51:44.820 --> 51:46.120
Proving historical occurrence

51:46.120 --> 51:47.600
is a vastly higher bar

51:47.600 --> 51:49.300
than proving mechanical feasibility.

51:49.860 --> 51:51.300
As Flyxion beautifully puts it,

51:51.600 --> 51:53.140
expanding the realm of the possible

51:53.140 --> 51:55.440
does not expand the realm of the historical.

51:55.960 --> 51:57.940
You can prove beyond a shadow of a doubt

51:57.940 --> 51:59.680
in a laboratory that a modern human

51:59.680 --> 52:00.940
could tune a stalagmite

52:00.940 --> 52:02.680
using a stretched animal hide in sand.

52:03.360 --> 52:04.480
You cannot easily prove

52:04.480 --> 52:06.280
that a Neanderthal did do that

52:06.280 --> 52:08.060
176,000 years ago.

52:08.340 --> 52:10.520
So we have gone incredibly deep

52:10.520 --> 52:11.360
into the cave today.

52:11.520 --> 52:13.920
We have explored the Euler-Bernoulli math,

52:14.340 --> 52:15.560
the physics of bending modes,

52:16.140 --> 52:18.340
the ingenious paleolithic tuning hacks,

52:18.500 --> 52:19.240
and the intense,

52:19.660 --> 52:21.420
falsifiable experimental gauntlet

52:21.420 --> 52:23.040
required to prove any of it.

52:23.440 --> 52:24.540
To summarize our journey,

52:25.380 --> 52:26.600
the Brunichel cave structures

52:26.600 --> 52:28.820
are an undeniable, monumental,

52:29.100 --> 52:30.200
grade three mystery.

52:30.200 --> 52:32.660
There is absolutely no debating

52:32.660 --> 52:34.980
that 176,000 years ago,

52:35.520 --> 52:36.240
deep underground,

52:36.760 --> 52:37.780
in total darkness,

52:38.280 --> 52:39.920
early Neanderthals were organizing,

52:40.180 --> 52:41.620
building, and controlling fire

52:41.620 --> 52:43.100
on a massive scale.

52:43.600 --> 52:44.820
Whether they were building

52:44.820 --> 52:46.720
a multi-ton stone piano

52:46.720 --> 52:47.360
to play melodies

52:47.360 --> 52:49.420
remains an incredibly tantalizing,

52:50.040 --> 52:51.600
beautifully testable hypothesis.

52:52.280 --> 52:53.980
And thanks to the physics of overtones

52:53.980 --> 52:55.600
and Flyxion's proposed

52:55.600 --> 52:56.880
experimental paradigms,

52:57.000 --> 52:58.400
we actually have a rigorous

52:58.400 --> 52:59.440
scientific roadmap

52:59.440 --> 53:00.580
to test it,

53:00.800 --> 53:01.820
rather than just guessing.

53:02.360 --> 53:03.860
It is a perfect example

53:03.860 --> 53:05.080
of how setting strict,

53:05.680 --> 53:06.980
rigorous scientific boundaries

53:06.980 --> 53:08.520
doesn't stifle creativity.

53:08.880 --> 53:10.620
It actually liberates us

53:10.620 --> 53:12.080
to ask better, deeper,

53:12.320 --> 53:13.440
more precise questions

53:13.440 --> 53:14.220
about our past.

53:14.420 --> 53:15.860
I really want to thank you,

53:16.040 --> 53:16.440
the listener,

53:16.660 --> 53:17.380
for joining us

53:17.380 --> 53:19.360
on this intense mental workout today.

53:19.960 --> 53:21.260
Understanding the strict boundaries

53:21.260 --> 53:22.520
of evidence parsing

53:22.520 --> 53:24.540
between what is mechanically plausible

53:24.540 --> 53:26.460
and what is historically proven

53:26.460 --> 53:27.980
really does make us

53:27.980 --> 53:29.200
better critical thinkers

53:29.200 --> 53:30.720
in every single aspect

53:30.720 --> 53:31.440
of modern life,

53:31.520 --> 53:32.600
from reading the news

53:32.600 --> 53:34.000
to understanding history.

53:34.620 --> 53:35.260
And I want to leave you

53:35.260 --> 53:36.120
with one final,

53:36.700 --> 53:38.160
deeply philosophical thought

53:38.160 --> 53:38.720
to mull over.

53:39.500 --> 53:40.580
The entire paper

53:40.580 --> 53:42.280
we just spent an hour discussing

53:42.280 --> 53:43.420
focuses heavily on

53:43.420 --> 53:44.500
whether the Neanderthals

53:44.500 --> 53:45.740
tuned the rocks

53:45.740 --> 53:48.120
to create music specifically,

53:48.280 --> 53:50.060
to create identifiable,

53:50.380 --> 53:51.440
stable, mathematically

53:51.440 --> 53:52.360
distinct pitches.

53:52.360 --> 53:54.220
But considering the heavily

53:54.220 --> 53:55.700
inharmonic, chaotic,

53:56.440 --> 53:57.520
intensely complex nature

53:57.520 --> 53:59.120
of striking dense calcite

53:59.120 --> 53:59.860
in a dark,

54:00.040 --> 54:02.060
highly reverberant cave environment,

54:02.580 --> 54:04.200
what if the concept of music

54:04.200 --> 54:05.680
as we modern humans

54:05.680 --> 54:06.460
understand it,

54:06.900 --> 54:08.080
with our neat,

54:08.320 --> 54:09.160
stable melodies,

54:09.520 --> 54:10.420
our harmonies,

54:10.500 --> 54:11.700
and our tuned scales,

54:12.300 --> 54:13.480
is entirely the wrong

54:13.480 --> 54:14.600
cognitive framework

54:14.600 --> 54:15.740
to apply to a species

54:15.740 --> 54:19.400
that lived 176,000 years ago?

54:19.740 --> 54:20.500
What if they weren't trying

54:20.500 --> 54:22.220
to play a recognizable tune at all?

54:22.780 --> 54:23.560
What if they were

54:23.560 --> 54:24.900
deliberately designing

54:24.900 --> 54:26.780
these massive physical structures

54:26.780 --> 54:28.440
to generate pure,

54:28.760 --> 54:29.960
overwhelming acoustic

54:29.960 --> 54:31.040
sensory overload?

54:31.580 --> 54:32.640
What if they were utilizing

54:32.640 --> 54:33.740
the chaotic,

54:34.060 --> 54:34.940
clashing partials

54:34.940 --> 54:35.800
of the calcite,

54:36.220 --> 54:37.160
not to play a song,

54:37.500 --> 54:38.580
but to induce altered

54:38.580 --> 54:39.300
trance states

54:39.300 --> 54:39.860
in the darkness?

54:40.420 --> 54:41.980
How would our null models,

54:42.220 --> 54:42.800
our math,

54:42.960 --> 54:44.280
and our entire understanding

54:44.280 --> 54:45.940
of prehistoric art change

54:45.940 --> 54:47.440
if the ultimate goal

54:47.440 --> 54:48.660
of the Neanderthal builders

54:48.660 --> 54:49.840
wasn't pitch,

54:49.840 --> 54:51.180
but raw,

54:51.580 --> 54:52.680
unadulterated resonance?

54:53.300 --> 54:53.660
That's something

54:53.660 --> 54:54.200
to think about.

54:54.680 --> 54:55.800
That thought gives me

54:55.800 --> 54:56.940
absolute chills.

54:57.100 --> 54:58.100
It completely reframes

54:58.100 --> 54:58.960
how we look at the past.

54:59.820 --> 55:00.500
Keep questioning

55:00.500 --> 55:01.340
the world around you,

55:01.420 --> 55:02.720
keep looking for the resonance

55:02.720 --> 55:03.400
and the noise,

55:03.400 --> 55:04.480
and we will catch you

55:04.480 --> 55:05.500
on our next deep dive.

